# Cross-game UX consistency

Players move between Platform shell remotes (LoL, WoW, future games). **The same job must feel the same** unless the game domain truly differs.

## Before inventing a UI/control pattern

1. **Search the sibling game and Template** for the same surface (roster, applications, media, LFG, workspace widgets, playground session, etc.).
2. **Reuse the same component family and interaction model** — e.g. if LoL roster uses Nebular `nb-select` + `gc-decision-prompt`, WoW must not ship a native `<select>` or a row of ad-hoc buttons for the same actions.
3. Prefer **Nebular** (and existing `@bari77/gc-*` primitives) already used on that surface in another Front. Do not “simplify” with raw HTML controls when the sibling already established a Nebular pattern.
4. Domain labels may differ (`captain` vs `leader`, team vs guild); **layout, affordances, confirmation flow, and empty/loading states** should stay aligned.

## When changing one game

- If you improve a shared interaction in one Front, **port the same UX to the structural twin** (LoL ↔ WoW) in the same change set when the surface exists there.
- Pillar / scaffold patterns also update **Games.Template** so `gc-create-game` clones inherit the coherent UX.
- Extract to **DevKit** when the control is game-agnostic; keep game-local only the permission matrix and copy.

## Anti-patterns

- Different control types for the same task across games (native select vs `nb-select`, icon-only kick vs rank combo, etc.).
- Skipping confirmation on one game when the sibling confirms destructive membership actions.
- Inventing a one-off layout for a sheet widget that already has a twin in another game.
